.TH TIRED 1 "September 2026" "tired 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tired \- turn a foreground command into a persistent systemd service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tired
[creation options] COMMAND [ARG...]
.br
.B tired create
[options] -- COMMAND [ARG...]
.br
.B tired
list | status NAME | show NAME | logs NAME | doctor [NAME]
.br
.B tired
start | stop | restart | enable | disable | edit | remove NAME
.br
.B tired rename
NAME NEW_NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
tired captures a command vector and working directory, proposes a typed service,
and installs it after review and administrator authorization when necessary.
Commands after the first workload token or -- belong to the workload.
The target is never executed during discovery.
Created services are ordinary systemd units and do not depend on tired remaining installed.
.SH SCOPE
System scope is the default; installation authority is separate from execution identity.
.B --user
selects the calling user's manager. Machine boot activation requires account lingering
and accessible workload files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --yes, --no-tui, --json
Approve ordinary headless changes, avoid curses, or select structured output.
Specific risks require repeatable --allow-risk CODE even with --yes.
.TP
.B --name NAME, --profile auto|none|ID
Select an exact service name or declarative profile. Names are never used to infer privilege.
.TP
.B --run-as USER, --group GROUP, --working-directory PATH
Select service identity and working directory. User scope cannot switch accounts.
.TP
.B --set FIELD=VALUE, --unset FIELD
Assign a typed advanced setting or explicitly inherit an optional setting.
.TP
.B --start, --no-start, --enable, --no-enable
Select immediate execution and future activation independently.
.TP
.B --restart POLICY, --restart-sec DURATION, --retry-policy persistent|limited
Select restart behavior. Persistent retries use a nonzero delay and no finite rate budget.
.TP
.B --nofile SOFT:HARD, --network none|network|online
Select descriptor limits or network ordering without modifying host limits or wait services.
.TP
.B --env KEY=VALUE, --pass-env KEY, --import-env-file PATH, --env-file PATH
Explicitly assign, capture, snapshot, or reference environment inputs.
No shell evaluation or blanket environment capture occurs.
.TP
.B --credential NAME=PATH
Add an application-compatible LoadCredential reference.
.TP
.B --hardening baseline
Explicitly select NoNewPrivileges=true, PrivateTmp=true and ProtectSystem=full.
The preset blocks privilege gains, isolates temporary files and makes /usr, /boot and /etc read-only.
Review application write requirements. Mount namespaces are required; nonroot user services cannot use this preset on the supported baseline.
Ordinary defaults are unchanged. Assigning a preset field again with --set or --unset is a duplicate scalar error.
.TP
.B --enable-linger
Explicitly request an account-level user-manager persistence change.
.TP
.B --now
With enable/disable, also change current runtime state.
.TP
.B --apply-mode restart|defer, --refresh-profile
Choose edit application or explicitly reevaluate profile recommendations.
.TP
.B --restore-managed
Explicit drift restoration; requires restore-drifted-unit acknowledgment and a private backup.
.TP
.B --keep-history
Retain private history when removing a service.
.TP
.B --output NEW_FILE, --include-sensitive
Export plan/show to a new private file. Sensitive exports require sensitive-export acknowledgment.
.TP
.B --offline, --unit
For plan, skip host validation or display proposed unit text.
.TP
.B --follow, --lines N, --since TIMESTAMP, --boot current|BOOT_ID
Journal selection; --json emits bounded NDJSON events.
.TP
.B --description TEXT, --type TYPE
Select description and process model: exec, simple, notify, forking or oneshot.
.TP
.B --start-limit-interval TIME, --start-limit-burst INTEGER
Select an explicit finite retry budget with limited retry policy.
.TP
.B --system, --quiet, --verbose, --color auto|always|never
Select system scope, human-output verbosity and presentation.
.TP
.B --allow-risk CODE, --sensitive-arg INDEX
Acknowledge a specific displayed risk or classify a command argument.
.TP
.B --dry-run
With create, produce a read-only plan.
.TP
.B --effective, --check-active
Inspect show fragment/drop-ins or return 7 from status when not running.
.TP
.B --active-state STATE, --enabled-state STATE, --profile ID, --search TEXT
Filter list observations and case-insensitive names.
.TP
.B --help, --version, --build-info
Display usage, version or compiler/linkage/baseline metadata.
.SH PROFILE AND SETTINGS COMMANDS
profiles list, show ID, explain -- COMMAND, validate FILE, install FILE --yes,
and remove ID --yes operate on declarative profiles.
config show and validate FILE inspect effective settings or validate one file.
Profile changes preserve service snapshots until edit --refresh-profile.
.SH REVIEW CONTROLS
H previews baseline hardening; Space selects it and Escape returns.
The question-mark key displays the selected field's origin, rationale and cited evidence.
P previews the unit; D shows typed differences and unified saved/disk/proposed comparisons.
S shows operation effects; E edits environment and K credentials; R reviews risks; C applies.
Editors block hidden edits while a resize message is shown and preserve pending input until the terminal is usable again.
.SH RECOVERY
.B tired recover
inspects durable transaction journals.
Resolve one transaction explicitly with --transaction UUID --resolution finish|rollback --yes.
An uncertain restart is observed rather than blindly repeated.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 completed; 1 internal failure; 2 invalid input; 3 unsupported environment;
4 authorization; 5 conflict; 6 rolled back; 7 installed/runtime failure;
8 recovery required; 9 not found; 10 cancelled; 130 interrupted before commit.
.SH FILES
/etc/systemd/system/*.service, /etc/tired/services/, /var/lib/tired/, /run/tired/.
User scope uses XDG configuration/state/runtime locations.
.SH SECURITY
The helper is neither setuid nor resident. It validates typed requests and derived destinations.
Classified secrets are redacted in normal output; arbitrary application logs can still contain secrets.
Stopping a networking service may interrupt the operator's connection.
.SH SEE ALSO
systemd.service(5), systemd.exec(5), systemctl(1), journalctl(1)
